<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hama is a city on the Orontes river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, famous for its great wooden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheels</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noria">norias</a>. As Hamath it was the capital of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdom until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> annexed it in 720 BCE, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was ruled by a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>, including the historian-prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abu al-Fida</a>. In 1982 the Syrian government crushed an uprising there, killing thousands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hama.jpg" alt="Hama" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hama,_Panoramic_view,_Syria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The ancient settlement of Hamath was occupied from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> it was the capital of a Syro-Hittite kingdom known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, which traded with Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. In 853 BCE its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urhilina">Irhuleni</a> and Hadadezer of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> led a coalition against the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Battle of Qarqar</a>, a battle that seems to have been closer to a draw. Around 720 BCE Sargon II razed the city after a failed revolt, resettled it with 6,300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and had its king flayed alive in Assyria.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> the city was renamed Epiphaneia after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 64 BCE. Five hundred Hamian archers were stationed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> from 120 CE. Muslim forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ubayda-ibn-al-jarrah">Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah</a> took the city in 638 or 639, and it regained its old name. The Crusader Tancred took it in 1108, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> recovered it in 1114. Two earthquakes in 1157 shattered it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> rebuilt its walls and, in 1172, its Great Mosque.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took Hama in 1175 and gave it four years later to his nephew al-Muzaffar Umar, beginning Ayyubid rule that lasted almost continuously until 1342. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked it in 1260. The historian and geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abu al-Fida</a> governed the city from 1310 to 1332. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1335. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered it in 1516. It became part of the French Mandate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after 1918, and a rising took place in the city in 1925 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hama lies on the Orontes 213 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and is the capital of the Hama Governorate. Its seventeen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noria">norias</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheels</a> up to 20 metres across, were used for irrigation and now serve as a symbol of the city. In February 1982 government forces under the president's brother Rifaat al-Assad put down an Islamist revolt in the city, shelling quarters held by the insurgents and razing part of the old city. Government forces are alleged to have executed thousands of prisoners and civilians afterwards.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It extinguished most of what remained, although <b>Hama</b> survived as a Mamluk vassal into the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muslim Brotherhood's uprising of 1976–1982 ended in the <b>Hama</b> massacre of 1982, the single deadliest act of violence by any state on its own people in modern Arab history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Horns of <b>Hama</b> on 13 April 1175 Saladin's outnumbered veterans surrounded and crushed the combined armies of Mosul and Aleppo; at the Sultan's Mound in 1176 the Zengids broke his left wing before his charge routed them, and Saif ad-Din barely escaped.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Kipchak boy captured in a Mongol invasion, was sold at the slave market of Sivas and again at <b>Hama</b>, came into the service of the sultan as-Salih Ayyub and in 1260 seized the throne, which he held until 1277; Barquq, a Circassian mamluk of the amir Yalbugha, became…</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Imber emigrated to Ottoman-ruled Palestine in 1882 and read the poem to the pioneers of the early Jewish villages, among them Rishon LeZion, Rehovot, Gedera and Yesud <b>Hama</b>'ala.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a></li><li><blockquote>Victory gave him Damascus, Homs and <b>Hama</b>; he then proclaimed himself king, and the Abbasid caliph named him Sultan of Egypt and Syria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
